("UMP5I3HM","Reciprocity & Justice","Recommended","Quantitative and qualitative course study","Ten service-learning courses at one urban U.S. university","125 undergraduate students","Tests relationships as a plausible mechanism connecting service-learning participation to civic orientation.","Positive relationships with professors, community partners, and peer mentors were associated with stronger reported intentions for future civic engagement.","The single-site sample was small, service experiences varied, associations are noncausal, and the study did not establish benefits for community partners.","Measure relationship quality across students, faculty, peers, and partners, but pair student attitudes with community-defined outcome evidence.",(3,2,1,1,1),"Full-text review",["relationships","civic engagement","mentoring","community partners"]),
("TNF6HBR4","Reciprocity & Justice","Core","Mixed-methods comparative course study","Three psychology classes at a Southern California university","125 undergraduates, predominantly students of color","Provides empirical evidence on combining service-learning with critical curriculum rather than assuming service automatically produces justice orientations.","Critical service-learning and critical-curriculum groups showed stronger civic orientations and lower social-dominance orientations than a traditional class; within-person results suggested some change over time.","Classes were not randomized, instructors and course designs may differ, and outcomes are attitudinal rather than evidence of community-level change.","Pair service with explicit critical curriculum and measure justice orientations, while avoiding claims that service alone transforms power relations.",(3,2,2,1,2),"Full-text review",["critical service-learning","social justice","civic engagement","mixed methods"]),
("FM2QLXHD","Reciprocity & Justice","Recommended","Conceptual essay and research agenda","Postsecondary service-learning access","No empirical sample","Makes access to service-learning itself an equity question, especially for students facing institutional and resource barriers.","The article identifies recruitment, navigation, time, and institutional processes as possible barriers and calls for qualitative inquiry centered on underrepresented students' experiences.","It proposes lines of inquiry rather than testing them; effectiveness and prevalence of the proposed barriers remain empirical questions.","Audit who can participate, who is recruited, hidden costs, scheduling, transport, and institutional navigation before calling a program inclusive.",(3,1,1,1,2),"Full-text review",["equity","access","underrepresented students","research agenda"]),
